  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nToday will be fair but cool after the chilly start, with highs in\nthe mid 60s to lower 70s for lower elevations. That's about 4-10 \ndegrees below average for valleys, and about 1-3 degrees below \naverage at the coast. The next storm Thursday will be pretty \nstrong, with a well-defined front moving through SoCal. Rain \nshould begin in the L.A. Basin Thursday morning, moving southeast \ninto Riverside and San Diego Counties through the afternoon. Rain \nwill be pretty intense along the front, and there is a small \nchance of thunderstorms. For both reasons, rainfall rates could \nreach 0.50-0.65 per hour, which could produce some urban flooding.\nSnow levels will start out high, and drop to about 6,500 feet by \nthe time the precipitation moves out. A few inches should \naccumulate above 7,500 feet, on the upper ski slopes around Big \nBear. Down at Big Bear Lake, little if any accumulations are \nexpected. But the timing of the cold air moving in with the \nmoisture are uncertain, so there's a range of potential snowfall \noutcomes. Winds will be strongest ahead of and along the front, \nwith gusts exceeding 25-30 mph for the coast and 40-55 mph in the \nmountains and adjacent desert slopes, where there's a wind \nadvisory. Showers will follow the front Thursday evening as the \nsnow level lowers, but then will eventually end Thursday night. \n\nFriday will start out quite cold, but clouds will clear to bring \nmostly sunny conditions. A secondary shortwave rotates through \nthe Great Basin Friday, but for now it doesn't appear to prolong \nthe precipitation, just postpone any warm up. A touch of offshore \nflow pays a visit Friday night and Saturday, but any brisk \noffshore winds will be quite isolated in foothills. High pressure \naloft doesn't build much, but enough to provide dry and slightly \nwarmer weather Friday through the weekend. Temperatures will \ncontinue below normal each of those days, with highs in the mid \n60s to lower 70s at lower elevations, upper 30s to mid 50s in the \nmountains, and 50s in the High Desert. Model guidance is starting \nto narrow the possibilities regarding the timing of the next \nprecipitation. \n\nA weak shortwave moves into the region Sunday, bringing light \nshowers Sunday night and Monday. Right now this system looks much \nweaker regarding precipitation amounts and winds. By Tuesday \nthere's some guidance showing a continuation of the unsettled, \noccasionally wet and windy pattern. But there are nearly equal \nnumbers of ensemble members showing a stronger ridge pattern for \ndry and warmer weather heading into the holiday.",
